      "kind": "figure",
      "heading": "Formula, frequency and doubling time",
      "body": "<style>\n.cf{box-sizing:border-box;max-width:1040px;margin:0 auto;font-family:var(--ow-font);color:var(--ow-fg);display:grid;gap:14px;grid-template-columns:1fr}\n@media (min-width:760px){.cf{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))}}\n.cf-c{padding:16px;border-radius:16px;background:var(--ow-panel);border:1px solid var(--ow-line)}\n.cf-h{margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ow-muted)}\n.cf-s{margin:0 0 12px;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--ow-muted);line-height:1.4}\n.cf-f{font-family:var(--ow-mono);font-size:clamp(19px,2.4vw,24px);margin:6px 0 12px}\n.cf-f sup{font-size:.6em}\n.cf ul.n{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;gap:5px;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--ow-muted)}\n.cf ul.n b{font-family:var(--ow-mono);color:var(--ow-fg)}\n.cf ol{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;gap:8px}\n.cf li.x,.cf ol li{display:grid;grid-template-columns:78px minmax(0,1fr) 54px;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:13.5px}\n.cf .lb{color:var(--ow-muted)}\n.cf .tr{height:14px;border-radius:99px;background:color-mix(in oklch,var(--ow-line) 70%,transparent);overflow:hidden}\n.cf .tr i{display:block;height:100%;border-radius:99px;background:var(--ow-accent)}\n.cf .pc{font-family:var(--ow-mono);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:13px;text-align:right}\n.cf table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}\n.cf th{text-align:left;font-weight:600;color:var(--ow-muted);padding:4px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--ow-line)}\n.cf td{padding:5px 0;border-bottom:1px solid color-mix(in oklch,var(--ow-line) 60%,transparent);font-family:var(--ow-mono)}\n.cf td:nth-child(2){color:var(--ow-accent);font-weight:600}\n.cf-debt{margin:12px 0 0;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--ow-muted)}\n.cf-debt b{color:var(--ow-fg)}\n</style>\n<div class=\"cf\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The compound interest formula; 1000 dollars at 5 percent for 30 years compounded yearly, monthly, daily and continuously; and rule of 72 doubling times against exact doubling times\">\n<div class=\"cf-c\"><p class=\"cf-h\">The formula</p>\n<div class=\"cf-f\">A = P(1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup></div>\n<ul class=\"n\"><li><b>P</b> what you start with</li><li><b>r</b> yearly rate, as a decimal</li><li><b>n</b> times per year interest is added</li><li><b>t</b> years</li><li><b>A</b> what you end with</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"cf-c\"><p class=\"cf-h\">How often it compounds</p><p class=\"cf-s\">$1,000 at 5% for 30 years</p>\n<ol><li><span class=\"lb\">Yearly</span><span class=\"tr\"><i style=\"width:94.0%\"></i></span><span class=\"pc\">$4,322</span></li><li><span class=\"lb\">Monthly</span><span class=\"tr\"><i style=\"width:97.1%\"></i></span><span class=\"pc\">$4,468</span></li><li><span class=\"lb\">Daily</span><span class=\"tr\"><i style=\"width:97.4%\"></i></span><span class=\"pc\">$4,481</span></li><li><span class=\"lb\">Continuous</span><span class=\"tr\"><i style=\"width:97.4%\"></i></span><span class=\"pc\">$4,482</span></li></ol>\n<p class=\"cf-debt\">Yearly to daily adds <b>$159</b>. Time matters far more: the same $1,000 left 60 years becomes <b>$18,679</b>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"cf-c\"><p class=\"cf-h\">Rule of 72</p><p class=\"cf-s\">Years to double: 72 &divide; rate vs exact</p>\n<table><tr><th>Rate</th><th>72 &divide; r</th><th>Exact</th></tr><tr><td>2%</td><td>36 yrs</td><td>35 yrs</td></tr><tr><td>4%</td><td>18 yrs</td><td>17.7 yrs</td></tr><tr><td>6%</td><td>12 yrs</td><td>11.9 yrs</td></tr><tr><td>8%</td><td>9 yrs</td><td>9 yrs</td></tr><tr><td>10%</td><td>7.2 yrs</td><td>7.3 yrs</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>6 yrs</td><td>6.1 yrs</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"cf-debt\">Debt runs the same curve: an unpaid balance at <b>24%</b> doubles in about <b>3 years</b>.</p></div>\n</div>"
